course: dictionary
lesson: 10265
title: Tooth and teeth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A tooth is one of the hard white parts in your mouth that you use to bite and chew your food. When you have more than one, you say teeth. The plural is special: tooth becomes teeth, and it does not add an s.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A tooth can also be one of the sharp points on a comb, a saw, or a gear. Read the words around it to find which meaning fits. «Brush your teeth» is about your mouth, but a saw's teeth are the sharp points along its blade.
explain: Context gives you clues. «A dog that growls, shows its teeth or goes stiff is warning you to stay away.» The dog is not smiling. Showing its teeth is a warning, so you know to keep your distance.
fact: Tooth means one hard white part in the mouth used to bite and chew food. Teeth is the plural.
fact: Children have baby teeth, also called milk teeth, which fall out and are replaced by adult teeth.
fact: Brush your teeth twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, and visit the dentist.
fact: Sugar feeds germs that make holes in teeth. These holes are called cavities.
fact: A «sweet tooth» means you like sweets very much. A «wisdom tooth» is the last adult tooth to grow.
exercise: «Picture a dentist's X-ray of a seven-year-old: the baby teeth sit in the mouth, and underneath them, packed into the jaw like eggs in a box, the grown-up teeth are already waiting.» What are the grown-up teeth doing? | *Already waiting under the baby teeth | Eaten by germs | Being brushed with toothpaste | Falling out of the mouth now
exercise: «A moving swing is heavy and fast at a child's head height and can knock you out or break teeth, so walk wide around swings.» What does this sentence tell you to do? | Hold the swing with your teeth | *Walk wide around swings | Swing as high as you can | Sit underneath the swing
exercise: Which word is the plural of tooth? | toothes | teeths | tooths | *teeth
test: Which word means one hard part in the mouth used to bite and chew food? | Lip | *Tooth | Jaw | Tongue
test: Which habit keeps your teeth healthy? | Eating sweets all day | *Brushing twice a day with fluoride toothpaste | Chewing pencils | Never visiting the dentist
exam: Read: «Sugar feeds germs that make holes in teeth.» What are these holes called? | Bubbles | Freckles | *Cavities | Splinters
